{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slightly rising) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude oscillations (spikes up to 85.66 and drops to 75.36). Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast range to capture these swings.",
    "Address the 'lag' in mean reversion: The model fails to anticipate the sharp upward spike (around day 4-6 of the forecast horizon) following a period of low sentiment or price dip. When sentiment history shows a transition from low (0.00) to high (1.00) or mixed signals, immediately adjust the forecast upward by 3-5 EUR to account for the delayed momentum.",
    "Prevent overshoot on the downside: After the peak, the model's slow decline is too gradual compared to the rapid crash in true prices. When the forecast horizon extends beyond the initial spike, apply a steeper downward slope correction to match the speed of the actual mean reversion.",
    "Sentiment-weighted bias adjustment: When recent sentiment history contains consecutive 1.00 values, the model's flat forecast is insufficient; add a positive bias of 2-4 EUR. Conversely, when sentiment drops to 0.00 or 0.33, the model's tendency to stay above 80 EUR is a systematic overestimation; reduce the baseline forecast by 2-3 EUR.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: The model performs best on the first 1-2 days but diverges significantly thereafter. For horizons > 5 days, do not rely on the model's linear extrapolation; instead, anchor the forecast to the historical mean of the last 18 days (approx. 81.5 EUR) and apply a dampened oscillation pattern rather than a monotonic trend."
  ]
}
